hen through the fast lab uh  associate professor tim inglis  christine who spoke to you before and  then  the rest of the team who have been  toiling away at all of these  uh innovations for many years now and  then you know  the university of western australia path  west the harry perkins institute and  demo fisher scientific who have been  gracious in allowing us to speak today  and to  communicate our findings to you so at  this point  i'll hand back over to our moderator  thank you dr carson and dr mueller for  your informative presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  okay let's go ahead and get started  let's see our first question  is is there scope for the dyes and  stains  used in microbial flow cytometry to be  further developed  ah yes great question and one that i'm a  little bit uh  rabidly passionate about myself so i did  mention briefly earlier about  you know how underprivileged poor  microbiologists are when it comes to new  dyes  and new tools you know with the changes  in cell envelope that come with  different strains of bacteria we can't  rely on antibodies as our primary method  of detection  and quite often you will find that  taking dyes out of the catalog that were  originally intended  for eukaryotic human cell use in  particular  they will not behave the same with  microorganisms  uh you'll find that you know a single  cellular living organism  does a whole bunch of things differently  than a cell within a tissue  and so but there are many great tools  out there  you do need to think about the way that  they're going to act  with the different physiology and the  different structures that go with it  and so there's there's both a lot of  potential in taking existing dyes off  the shelf and repurposing them  if you understand the