asier to incorporate flow  cytometry  into the methods that you want to use to  study a particular problem  because there isn't the regulation and  the  validation requirements around using the  instruments  and the techniques and i that  sort of frees people up to explore i  guess  the applications that they may be able  to use flow cytometry for  i think eventually they will slow cetera  will be used  in a greater role in clinical  microbiology laboratories  but there's a lot of work between where  we are now  and getting to that stage meanwhile you  know it's very easy  to apply flow cytometry in research  laboratories  thank you dr carson okay we've time for  one more question  why isn't flow cytometry used more  widely  in diagnostic microbiology  well as karen has articulated very  clearly  you know the biology of the organisms  involved  in the kinds of tests that we need is  quite complex  the um in the clinical microbiology  laboratory the specimens that come in  you know the sample matrix they vary  considerably depending on what  specimen you're dealing with you know  urine is very different to a peritoneal  dialysis fluid  which is very different from a blood  sample and the complexity  of the those sample matrices  needs you need to consider all of that  when you're designing your tests  and unless you are designing from the  ground up  with the intent to use it in a  diagnostic  application it's very easy to take wrong  terms along the way  so the potential for a flow cytometry to  be used  more in diagnosis certainly exists  that now needs to be translated into you  know accurate sensitive specific  diagnostic tests that can meet all the  validation requirements  and that's not a trivial task uh you  know it's achievable  but it would require a lot of very  focused  dedicated work to achieve that outcome  well thank you again dr christine carson  and dr karen mulverney for your time  today  we would also like to thank labrutz and  our sponsor thermo fisher scientific for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today  and further interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by our speakers  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  labyrinths will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time
 

